nterval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,6,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ee6eff-7680-5065-82e1-bd6bd0dad80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351645} {:answer/id #uuid "3df550b1-ff9b-545b-9666-338acd7370fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352139} {:answer/id #uuid "79e9ea4f-1d37-5972-98cc-68f3ad90cb9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352627} {:answer/id #uuid "8d26506b-d90a-59d2-a537-591cf8160473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352797} {:answer/id #uuid "932e6c56-fcd2-5abd-839d-21c49b27f887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d26506b-d90a-59d2-a537-591cf8160473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352797} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4c3fdb-5882-52c8-bc3f-740b0e59a29e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02a56550-64c7-56dd-a9e8-7fca3f195d30" :question/math-academy-id "q-88208" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001a1d3e-ef91-522d-a515-ff284b597a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351562} {:answer/id #uuid "28e1a538-9cc7-5ac7-934f-5f28060b9a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351947} {:answer/id #uuid "6660b7ce-f2bd-5d99-aaca-caa0ac85b037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352468} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cc7b45-3c19-5126-a70d-f8a33895e84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353133} {:answer/id #uuid "fd34c9d1-fd44-549d-8eef-c8b5598bfa72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6cc7b45-3c19-5126-a70d-f8a33895e84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353133} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef508f95-e026-5544-aa9a-0df646d38b38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ebd12802-ac6e-5b21-a42e-2abc0f87cbb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-88689" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0479fce1-8cec-502b-b667-3e77917efc14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351608} {:answer/id #uuid "1118664a-0e36-539f-a168-92623bcd480a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351711} {:answer/id #uuid "7587490a-b51d-556b-aa82-9944ed1f432c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352601} {:answer/id #uuid "a28d4852-8403-54d8-b565-82317cc8a21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352958} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c9fb5f-7d37-553e-91a8-6daddd32ed24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7587490a-b51d-556b-aa82-9944ed1f432c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352601} :answer-field/id #uuid "471fda10-6ad5-59ad-8bbe-df4504994973" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6956ed56-9d47-53cf-b35c-82d26f52b6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-88692" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)({e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20d9e1c6-dc1c-5a43-adc5-2474161385ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351861} {:answer/id #uuid "3bab63f8-8512-5133-9361-f49e7306e19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352118} {:answer/id #uuid "541158ab-6a25-5604-9637-0d671abc4d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352315} {:answer/id #uuid "5545d3aa-c480-5830-a9a6-3e1301b21241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352321} {:answer/id #uuid "61f0cc41-9f6b-5309-8d54-2c88a34ddd00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20d9e1c6-dc1c-5a43-adc5-2474161385ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351861} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d7eeef4-c9b4-5b33-a4a2-35fe73868d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "857f38ae-b398-5bec-99e3-9572a98abf10" :question/math-academy-id "q-88695" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}(x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x){e}^{-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-7}^{7}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$  is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{2}{e}^{(-x)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=(x+1)^{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =((-x)+1)^{3} \\\\  & =(1-x)^{3} \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05050cca-9d3f-5e1a-bf1e-42483f04be1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354696} {:answer/id #uuid "0680b01d-e915-5204-b1fc-bf55823d8530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354710} {:answer/id #uuid "328751de-7e85-5022-9321-6259e28b4a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355109} {:answer/id #uuid "95e4eb45-9a4f-5d44-a87c-d2c5b4b86185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355983} {:answer/id #uuid "9d665343-788f-50b1-9457-0a3d9dc6a8c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95e4eb45-9a4f-5d44-a87c-d2c5b4b86185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355983} :answer-field/id #uuid "393e19e3-2e12-542c-907f-3a6fa2957616" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ea94d170-9b47-53c8-b5f9-b4a6837f2064" :question/math-academy-id "q-88698" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+2) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{5}+(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{5}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{(-x)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f111a41-5caa-5d3e-9565-045ae103484a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355073} {:answer/id #uuid "5699cfaf-8b6b-508a-9a92-4618ab70e94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355438} {:answer/id #uuid "aa39c924-d027-5d40-bd71-aecc9b93b789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356171} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcf3596-bf98-50ec-aca3-f9afe414fcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356466} {:answer/id #uuid "f014d13f-2ec9-5872-a2c3-1d45432aac93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdcf3596-bf98-50ec-aca3-f9afe414fcca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356466} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d0afcc-ea38-58b9-be89-5c71b67a5130" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89d077ef-e867-5965-84f4-b7f7051e5ef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-89206" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The exact time (not rounded to integers) that a full charge of a phone battery lasts\n- The number of pets owned by a randomly selected family in a town\n- The height of a randomly selected plant in a nursery" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does not describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,….$\n\n- Statement III does not describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc945cd-e0a6-5e86-adc0-7a5446b2a41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355050} {:answer/id #uuid "946e3569-88e7-5e1b-88fa-a93871220bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355971} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d1e7c0-f30e-5e03-ba20-ced2f5de00e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356229} {:answer/id #uuid "d39b7d0b-0005-5346-9793-d0d086cf68b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356513} {:answer/id #uuid "f03bc8d1-6109-574a-817b-84faa4cb038b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1d1e7c0-f30e-5e03-ba20-ced2f5de00e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356229} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd6b99f6-a1a6-5460-bc79-7d630213eba0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3640d184-7917-5cae-8823-08846078aa32" :question/math-academy-id "q-89354" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of baskets made by a basketball player attempting three free throws if the probability of scoring any free throw is $65%$\n- The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine\n- The number of views of a video on a popular video channel" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of baskets in $3$ throws can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $3$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of defective items in a random sample of $100$ items can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $100$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of views cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "092acafc-ffd5-5797-a759-01e76168f137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354751} {:answer/id #uuid "270480e1-5a86-5661-af71-0bec66228073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354992} {:answer/id #uuid "3488dcd2-c958-5c0b-b078-5ce8fa57abe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355131} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0137aa-9dca-508c-9c39-7891f7921d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355744} {:answer/id #uuid "f74553d8-8176-5029-aa02-ed6216a17c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "270480e1-5a86-5661-af71-0bec66228073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354992} :answer-field/id #uuid "0379c6af-a9ef-537d-966a-5cc9b7220b21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad260dd3-b7b0-51a7-8aee-33250f0e09b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89355" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of boys in a family of $3$ children\n- The number of red cars passing through an avenue in an hour\n- The number of pizzas sold by a pizzeria in a day" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of boys can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $3$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although being red can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of pizzas sold cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28ba961e-6fdc-5e12-b72a-e28b7953c9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355005} {:answer/id #uuid "81f4db4c-c77d-544f-a284-a0da09109993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355807} {:answer/id #uuid "980597e3-98dd-5bed-9a8a-f6281df9fc1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356005} {:answer/id #uuid "a79aacd6-b097-5294-b62b-a3243e2d485c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356148} {:answer/id #uuid "e6253dc2-ed52-573c-8e5d-feed2590c7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28ba961e-6fdc-5e12-b72a-e28b7953c9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355005} :answer-field/id #uuid "64196aaf-e186-5dc6-91cf-d3e48cbe8149" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebe79c85-c57b-513c-b661-1636edd13781" :question/math-academy-id "q-89866" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y-4x}{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,5)$\n- $(-2,-4)$\n- $(-1,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given points. We substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,5)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(1,5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(5)-4(1)}{1} \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-4)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(-2,-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-4)-4(-2)}{(-2)} \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-3)} & ={(\\frac{2y-4x}{x})|}_{(-1,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-3)-4(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "699b4f2b-3092-5e5f-989d-32731e3e50fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355595} {:answer/id #uuid "8a38d130-ca40-5c0b-bd38-fd7f082dd319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355887} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a08319-5568-516b-b048-42e09b5f5fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356359} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f7091a-44e1-5279-a918-dd1b0480f1eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356634} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a6953a-1f09-5bb7-9de0-39ec21575c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9a6953a-1f09-5bb7-9de0-39ec21575c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356845} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b3acfa-7621-54d0-b4dd-c8d64b08551d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a6c55b2-0dbc-5417-9666-4eed70a77fd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-89869" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+{y}^{2}=x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=y(x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)(1-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(4,-2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(4,-2)} \\\\  & =(-2)(4-(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,2)} & ={y(x-{y}^{2})|}_{(2,2)} \\\\  & =(2)(2-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0745ca-fd31-5a4a-9128-29038ae2082d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354774} {:answer/id #uuid "2d5ba477-a268-5b88-ba09-26b15a321de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355054} {:answer/id #uuid "389eb858-d15e-5e00-80e0-f72945ee7bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355170} {:answer/id #uuid "be2ab531-05c2-564f-85b3-48a586d5b3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the points are the stationary points of the function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356330} {:answer/id #uuid "f365b82b-9999-5556-9779-931635dbdd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be2ab531-05c2-564f-85b3-48a586d5b3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the points are the stationary points of the function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356330} :answer-field/id #uuid "96db8478-9082-5202-aa63-8587656a562f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e87b95d9-4b1d-5eb0-8c89-8d155a899eb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-89871" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(2,-2)$\n- $(e,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={e}^{y}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(1,0)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(1,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(2,-2)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(2,-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2}}{4} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(e,1)} & ={(\\frac{{e}^{y}}{{x}^{2}})|}_{(e,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the points are the stationary points of the function.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157363 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e646aab-a614-5cbb-ae6d-b8a6cd45b32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355779} {:answer/id #uuid "b221760d-5291-5258-a8d8-9679b91f55ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356232} {:answer/id #uuid "d55cc5c9-7863-56c5-8849-8d14aea7ec75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356531} {:answer/id #uuid "e65f44eb-8340-5876-b33f-bd7adb11becf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356679} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8239db-7070-5b31-ae68-35838447b44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b221760d-5291-5258-a8d8-9679b91f55ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356232} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c381ad-8773-562d-b7b6-498567a363dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b92d6550-a190-5c3e-8b25-14493984cf60" :question/math-academy-id "q-89872" :question/problem "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(1,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(-1,-1)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,0)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}-2(0) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b3dd19-4961-5122-bf00-c223807d468b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354839} {:answer/id #uuid "259e3773-d741-550e-a66c-c8b52d72ddb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354980} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6d4ba4-a48c-5829-ab6c-c36d287b1444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355064} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4b4f23-7218-5988-bcb0-690ab5ea457d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356076} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd2f0b1-8ab5-5078-b829-4976446ae37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13b3dd19-4961-5122-bf00-c223807d468b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354839} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ff383a1-2384-5d1d-917e-554a73550720" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e244cae-49ac-5727-b109-5857ab506f84" :question/math-academy-id "q-89887" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\pi ,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\pi ,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+0-2 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi ,1)}=1\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\pi ,1)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(\\pi ,1),$ therefore we cannot have a local maximum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\pi ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57884b4d-1639-5f1e-b400-af64eb5ea912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355443} {:answer/id #uuid "6431313c-bbaf-57f8-9312-067ffd4df9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355557} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ab2fff-b8df-51c0-a7bb-ec98e428bcae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356244} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f7af4a-503f-52f9-a774-7427c293cb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356370} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cb5dd5-d954-5008-8f85-3e2711a89ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4cb5dd5-d954-5008-8f85-3e2711a89ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356799} :answer-field/id #uuid "990b536a-572c-548e-a308-3781737259c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0272b49a-4d96-50b3-ac67-e22a2911e738" :question/math-academy-id "q-89888" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0+4-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "742fab62-2d06-536a-ac43-4f705d054ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355685} {:answer/id #uuid "772057a0-6006-533a-b76c-08f1788e29df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355710} {:answer/id #uuid "9763ad30-3bcd-56f9-87d2-9159a1e1453f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355996} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0a453f-9749-56ee-8c0b-5a351b8285c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356041} {:answer/id #uuid "e7bb0d9c-8915-576b-94ef-41b96da3d748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7bb0d9c-8915-576b-94ef-41b96da3d748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356690} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1744326-c6c0-5dec-8ce6-3be346000609" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7cbbdaa7-7656-5f0c-adc2-8162521aa896" :question/math-academy-id "q-89894" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(4xy)|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =4(0)(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)\\cdot y+4x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =4y+4x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4y+4x(4xy) \\\\  & =4y(1+4{x}^{2}) \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)} & ={(4y(1+4{x}^{2}))|}_{(0,1)} \\\\  & =4(1)(1+4(0)^{2}) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(0,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20b40427-1854-5d65-a875-90b8922bec0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354941} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5b8f76-b899-5196-8c81-3b1f6ecc7450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355014} {:answer/id #uuid "62445271-fa5b-5f4a-a394-3e7335d8d9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355541} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1d4aa4-46ae-5c3e-acee-016a937cd76d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355767} {:answer/id #uuid "9a07a36e-50e6-5a1b-b1df-190c1d448433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a07a36e-50e6-5a1b-b1df-190c1d448433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356032} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf30d534-523f-547a-b2c2-d1f936770b30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e18f8cd9-b1f2-5b99-9f93-cbb0a6aa2a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-89895" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,-3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,-3)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,-3)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,-3),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={(\\frac{x}{y})|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{(-3)} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-x\\cdot \\frac{x}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={(\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}})|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{2}-{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(0,-3).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)}<0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local maximum (not a local minimum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a006e89-6bfd-5881-8622-7abd7b856944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355188} {:answer/id #uuid "5884af0e-6bf0-574f-bbb1-057bde96b721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355451} {:answer/id #uuid "71c7ae41-81f6-5f88-8ecb-01a5a8944ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355667} {:answer/id #uuid "d442c729-d5bf-5ec6-b22e-42e8811f3e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356518} {:answer/id #uuid "da411289-68c6-5346-9406-300562a21986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5884af0e-6bf0-574f-bbb1-057bde96b721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355451} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b9dbd08-85c4-5ab1-967b-fad7f72dde62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a2bd93d-e29f-5907-89bd-df4c7adaca45" :question/math-academy-id "q-90582" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n- $f(y)={y}^{2}+y$\n- $g(x,y)={x}^{3}y+{y}^{4}$\n- $h(x,y,z)=xyz$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(y)$  is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2.$ However, it also contains a term ($y$) that is of degree $1.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $2.$\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4.$ It is a polynomial of degree $4,$ and all of its terms are of degree $4.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ with no terms of degree other than $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fca8ddc-7b3c-51d2-b64f-d0fb70ab2fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355078} {:answer/id #uuid "517104fe-df7f-5fbc-b94d-13d3b39ae86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355389} {:answer/id #uuid "8058d5e5-0436-5775-9bc3-ca5ca0fe5301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355793} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a7fa1e-b801-51f7-8abe-d3d3838848b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356555} {:answer/id #uuid "d9be5a79-f1aa-508c-9f4e-7e190bdd6060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8058d5e5-0436-5775-9bc3-ca5ca0fe5301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355793} :answer-field/id #uuid "5646b317-7bdc-5c51-a5b2-4a57a8ed8747" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357065}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0704d357-8cb1-56bb-b120-20ad34cf98ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-9253" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(-1,0)$\n- $(0,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(-1,0),(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ae2dc0-2c48-57fe-b77f-9f67bdd2e46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354715} {:answer/id #uuid "2e42ae41-89c0-5b47-bbb6-fb998225a484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355061} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3683ca-db72-5783-ae9e-4b338e571426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355337} {:answer/id #uuid "7392534c-9571-5b57-9ed2-8040fdba11dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355683} {:answer/id #uuid "8c59e2a9-7838-53b2-bb73-74bd5d9dd03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b3683ca-db72-5783-ae9e-4b338e571426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355337} :answer-field/id #uuid "224c6656-d13d-552e-80a0-a1cdf090e6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da6f4133-24dc-5140-aaa0-0ea075dfe3f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-9255" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(14,6),$ and $(9,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(5,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007f0237-ad10-55bc-96ed-537c0b924d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354660} {:answer/id #uuid "11fcff98-4636-54be-aca6-915931063b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354827} {:answer/id #uuid "14c32ab5-b09a-5fdd-82ca-b53500065288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354845} {:answer/id #uuid "795d0faa-7625-51e4-bb91-86f69d1df25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355725} {:answer/id #uuid "bcab29af-ae5a-59e7-a74f-d9e3ee2a1b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "795d0faa-7625-51e4-bb91-86f69d1df25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355725} :answer-field/id #uuid "b61999bc-41a8-5be5-8d80-0cb905616eba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1174578d-6631-5fcf-81bf-729c1fac3da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-9258" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(4,-1),(17,-3),$ and $(10,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(-1,1)$\n- $(5,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-0}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112c3c85-bec9-56e4-a9ee-09149d0d3fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354821} {:answer/id #uuid "1d675587-ad44-5c63-aa9d-fd8d11f41890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354918} {:answer/id #uuid "31a76e84-b983-5def-ad11-edb615520444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355099} {:answer/id #uuid "94022cd9-9367-58e6-8da4-3b37e31b023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355969} {:answer/id #uuid "de106381-5201-5a91-8220-244c9c95b9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d675587-ad44-5c63-aa9d-fd8d11f41890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354918} :answer-field/id #uuid "5256f032-d750-537f-8b01-5c6ba0859d71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f585952-0c84-59bf-89fe-d6813830d204" :question/math-academy-id "q-9260" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(-21,0),(21,0),$ and $(0,3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(0,-3)$\n- $(3,1)$\n- $(-3,3)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-7-0}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}-21 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-3,0),(3,0),(0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0430e1ba-777f-5b9f-a671-927dfc1b7336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354690} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2cf3b6-602b-5cf3-a7f8-c3947a72b871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355336} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb82dbf-e096-5819-a946-dff85100e7fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356046} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6826c9-c659-5821-8dd5-1e16666c7bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356438} {:answer/id #uuid "f70ee03f-ba96-5e14-8ab8-61d5ee69963c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0430e1ba-777f-5b9f-a671-927dfc1b7336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354690} :answer-field/id #uuid "33db2135-ab30-5b0e-910b-265e95142ae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a755a352-feaa-5539-8e01-5e0ad683e5ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-9261" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(-2,2),(-5,4),(-3,2),$ and $(0,0).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(2,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3-2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2),(-1,2),(-1,0),(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357526 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1764762c-de15-580d-a3dc-d3428413e244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354874} {:answer/id #uuid "22932542-91c1-5542-b992-39df3e777a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354956} {:answer/id #uuid "d5368fdb-4e16-5d03-a93d-b845ab3a5e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356529} {:answer/id #uuid "d60c7df3-68ec-5105-8af1-7b52c6ddc66a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356539} {:answer/id #uuid "f142f0c1-47d7-5f91-bfd0-c8a4dd1bd3e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5368fdb-4e16-5d03-a93d-b845ab3a5e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356529} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d071199-1bd1-5314-aa05-d2b5389ab6b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c32a27cb-e2ec-5eaa-a1cb-1ea5655b2b83" :question/math-academy-id "q-9262" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(2,-5),(3,-6),(4,-8),$ and $(3,-7).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(1,3)$\n- $(3,3)$\n- $(2,3)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,2),(3,3),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217a9fbf-12ec-585e-8124-936aef700082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354948} {:answer/id #uuid "6211568c-3621-5b6b-b920-7eea97b2bfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355537} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad89b6f-5afc-54c9-ac6e-63308c1675f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356040} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b17d8e-dca4-5f59-a384-e7b13e8a71c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356125} {:answer/id #uuid "e1efb66b-43bd-55b1-b422-8000bbb8b8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ad89b6f-5afc-54c9-ac6e-63308c1675f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356040} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7510a5-9947-5806-929b-8b2b4d20ca58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a6a4708-c258-5b5f-8c4c-fa774844e5ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-9263" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(1,1),(0,0),(-1,1),$ and $(0,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4c6020-b712-524b-9dd4-fd76dcbfa2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354768} {:answer/id #uuid "3238cf73-aac5-5d48-a4bf-5a341a260092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355104} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c1e410-6a03-537a-a5e6-27c78ec91c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356228} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7306bd-678c-5c17-87a4-3a1c20658b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356339} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8193d6-5731-53cd-b9e3-025378afb6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b4c6020-b712-524b-9dd4-fd76dcbfa2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354768} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4d1570-a4c9-589c-9f25-2961695bfcd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "56746454-856c-5001-bcf8-93772c0debce" :question/math-academy-id "q-9264" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(0,0),(-2,-2),(-4,0),$ and $(-2,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(-2,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,0),(2,0),(2,2),(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dded7e-582e-5ec1-b987-ce936b8780b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354719} {:answer/id #uuid "109b309a-f087-5d7e-b2c9-056fb6f7ebfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354813} {:answer/id #uuid "33a58d4a-c0e7-5328-895a-91ecd0236e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355118} {:answer/id #uuid "be29496e-4ea5-5052-a05a-c4eb5f330612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356329} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c070e4-1e49-54fc-8ce2-efbc05e5f661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be29496e-4ea5-5052-a05a-c4eb5f330612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356329} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc13cff-2d66-5429-a5b3-63a4f9a7d12d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95ad491b-4ee0-5e7b-8a0c-33d2b7cf1e9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93715" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}+y=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=-x,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{′}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}+y=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "391ab15a-7637-50ca-8ad9-0d06dbc7db48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355177} {:answer/id #uuid "72e6cafb-5568-5033-8094-3bf866d01d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355675} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4f30a5-521c-543d-a973-483997a9b515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355930} {:answer/id #uuid "dae9edb1-a5bb-5c1f-81bc-26e592fe7d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356590} {:answer/id #uuid "f619a56c-197a-5bb8-8619-ce0c36b06cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dae9edb1-a5bb-5c1f-81bc-26e592fe7d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356590} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0e5c6e2-b88c-50be-9533-78e52f70d7c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6e8b60c-c6c9-5753-b254-94e3e9635292" :question/math-academy-id "q-93721" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x$\n- $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\frac{1}{x},b(x)=1,c(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=0,c(x)=17,$ and $f(x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aed7340-5f0e-5165-ab13-314353711b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355333} {:answer/id #uuid "8963ef11-348f-5e5f-8b9b-9f406cee908f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355880} {:answer/id #uuid "97fba95d-9f14-57b0-a978-2376e948fa78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356004} {:answer/id #uuid "bf821222-3076-59f2-8a78-9a7dfb62f4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356340} {:answer/id #uuid "e046d0c3-8ff2-5bd0-8adf-c6c8f1901ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8963ef11-348f-5e5f-8b9b-9f406cee908f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355880} :answer-field/id #uuid "e67b1e1e-56ae-5451-a8ba-26dd0254b3a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "225f8bdf-5da4-5137-973e-d6466e5f7f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93985" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5a30c2-2f06-5753-8caf-ff9645089df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354797} {:answer/id #uuid "47b9ccab-3108-5c8f-9b8c-5464d8508940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355301} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf821ab-99cc-5436-8de5-50227fd28ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355350} {:answer/id #uuid "543cd51b-5184-59d0-ac0b-707a18d83b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355413} {:answer/id #uuid "7c862cd4-aca7-5b9a-8fc3-e8126c91b2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf821ab-99cc-5436-8de5-50227fd28ea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355350} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9482b40-d3eb-5583-9a45-f6fae5b035a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc49e39d-dc41-5d92-84ab-b1310e571077" :question/math-academy-id "q-93988" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8a088d-38ed-529a-a0ad-6f8904a93baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354791} {:answer/id #uuid "1171e5a0-776e-5f64-b0f4-c6ac65970f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354823} {:answer/id #uuid "375c1745-5919-5823-b480-6bcd0cf9f315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355162} {:answer/id #uuid "9c727cd7-331c-5b79-a010-330c635aef7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356055} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e19068-c9c1-5a4a-b765-7eb8327e35ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e19068-c9c1-5a4a-b765-7eb8327e35ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356780} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a19c8ce-0f70-556e-8f7f-0eec3b04e312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a5f4b21-f8de-5f12-8b8c-c4be1021742e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93993" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{5} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\""}]}
